<h2>Why Commercial Property Claims Are Different</h2>
<h3>Low-slope roofs</h3>
<p>Many commercial buildings have low-slope roofs covered with TPO, modified bitumen, built-up roofing, or metal panels. Hail damage to these systems can be subtle. Fractures in membranes, crushed insulation boards beneath the surface, and dented metal seams may require core samples, moisture surveys, or close-up inspection to document. Rooftop HVAC units, skylights, and flashings add more line items.</p>
<h3>Multiple buildings and locations</h3>
<p>Apartment complexes and business parks may have dozens of roofs and elevations. Each building needs its own documentation, and the policy may apply blanket or per-building limits and deductibles.</p>
<h3>Business income and extra expense</h3>
<p>When a covered loss shuts down or slows operations, business income coverage may help replace lost net income and continuing expenses during the restoration period. Extra expense coverage may help with costs to keep operating, such as temporary space. These claims usually require financial records, not just repair estimates.</p>
<h3>Code and permit requirements</h3>
<p>Corpus Christi adopted the 2021 International Building Code and related codes, with local amendments, effective August 1, 2023, according to the <a href="https://www.corpuschristitx.gov/department-directory/development-services/construction-codes-and-ordinances/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">city's Development Services department</a>. Repairs inside the TDI catastrophe area also involve windstorm certification. Ordinance or law coverage, if included, may pay for increased code-compliance costs up to its limit. See our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page.</p>

