<h2>Common Disputes on Commercial Claims</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Repair versus replace on low-slope roofs.</strong> An insurer may propose patching areas of a membrane roof when wet insulation or widespread damage supports a larger scope.</li>
<li><strong>Pre-existing condition arguments.</strong> Older commercial roofs and salt-air corrosion give insurers room to argue that damage predates the storm. Maintenance records and prior inspection reports help.</li>
<li><strong>Coinsurance penalties.</strong> If the building was underinsured relative to its value, some policies reduce the payment. Policy terms and values at the time of loss matter.</li>
<li><strong>Business income calculations.</strong> Disagreements over the length of the restoration period, projected revenue, and which expenses continued are common.</li>
<li><strong>Wind versus water.</strong> After hurricanes, wind policies and flood policies may each attribute damage to the other peril. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-storm-surge-damage-claims-texas-coast/">wind vs. storm surge damage</a> explains how this works.</li>
</ul>

<h2>First Steps for Business Owners After a Loss</h2>
<p>The first few days after a storm or fire shape the rest of the claim. Before cleanup crews and roofers start work, take these steps where it is safe to do so:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Protect the property.</strong> Most policies require reasonable steps to prevent further damage, such as tarping roof openings, boarding windows, and extracting water. Keep every invoice.</li>
<li><strong>Capture conditions before they change.</strong> Photograph roofs, rooftop equipment, interiors, inventory, and exterior elevations. Damage to membranes and insulation can be harder to prove once temporary repairs cover it.</li>
<li><strong>Notify every insurer that may be involved.</strong> On the coast, that may include a property carrier, a TWIA or private windstorm policy, and a flood insurer. Tenants should notify their own carriers too.</li>
<li><strong>Track lost income and extra costs from day one.</strong> Keep daily sales records, note closures, and save receipts for temporary space, equipment rentals, and overtime.</li>
<li><strong>Do not discard damaged stock or equipment</strong> until it has been documented and the insurer has had a chance to inspect it.</li>
</ul>
<p>If hail was involved, our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> page explains how hail evidence is typically gathered on metal and membrane roofs.</p>

<h2>Documentation for Commercial Claims</h2>
<ol>
<li>Photos and video of every building, roof section, and interior space affected</li>
<li>Roof plans, building drawings, and maintenance or warranty records</li>
<li>Prior inspection reports and any roof surveys</li>
<li>Inventory and equipment lists with purchase records</li>
<li>Profit and loss statements, tax returns, and payroll records for business income</li>
<li>Invoices for emergency services, temporary repairs, and extra expenses</li>
<li>Leases, especially the insurance and repair provisions</li>
</ol>
<p>Our guide to <a href="/document-commercial-property-damage-after-storm/">documenting commercial property damage after a storm</a> goes into more detail, and the <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> covers the basics.</p>

<h2>How Our Commercial Claim Service Works</h2>
<p>We start with the policy, because commercial forms and endorsements vary widely. Then we inspect each affected building, using drone and roof photos, Matterport 3D imaging where useful, and detailed measurements. We prepare an Xactimate estimate and coordinate with roofers, engineers, and other specialists when the scope calls for it. On business income claims, we work with your accountant to organize the financial records the insurer will request. Throughout the claim, we handle communication with the insurer's adjusters and consultants and keep you informed.</p>
<p>A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster cannot promise a particular result, and some disputes, such as coverage litigation, call for a Texas attorney. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. If the insurer has already issued an estimate, consider an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a>, or request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> to talk through your commercial loss.</p>
